the main causes of death and premature  death worldwide are from non  communicable chronic diseases including  cancer cardiovascular disease diabetes  and chronic respiratory disease and the  main causes of those disease are  behaviors which include smoking  excessive consumption of alcohol food  and physical inactivity earlier this  year the World Health Assembly pledged  to reduce those diseases by twenty-five  percent by 2025 a laudable ambition but  that's going to require major shifts in  behavior worldwide there is common  interventions that have been tried over  the last two three decades involved  giving people information about the  risks from engaging in these health  damaging behaviors it becomes  unsurprising when we look at recent  findings from psychology and  neuroscience that these aren't having  the effects that people expected because  these latest findings are telling us  that much of our behavior is not subject  to reflective processes it's actually  occurring mainly automatically without  people's awareness and it doesn't mean  that we're behaving randomly but rather  our behavior is being guided by what  we've done in the past or by the  activation of ideas that appeal to us or  actually behavior that in our  environment is going to be the most easy  thing to do  various experiments show that food that  is placed further away is less likely to  be selected and some experiments we have  quite a precise idea of how large the  effect is say food that is more than 10  inches away from somebody is twelve  percent less likely to be selected  another approach is to try to change the  association's that are activated by  environmental stimuli say one recent  example that's been much discussed as  being the removal of branding on  cigarettes and say we know that the  branding on cigarettes increases the  attractiveness particularly young people  of cigarettes and similarly we know that  the branding on fast food increases the  attractiveness to children as well as  adults so removing that kind of branding  can reduce the attractiveness of  unhealthier products and the trick now  is to find ways of increasing the  attractiveness of healthier products so  one particular example a study conducted  with three year old children was able to  increase the chances that they tried a  lentil stew by just changing the name so  the lentil stew is described either as  healthy stew or as teddy bears porridge  and when it was described as teddy bears  porridge forty percent of these three  year olds tried it compared with just I  think the twenty-five percent who tried  it when it was described as a health  east you an example of an intervention  that changes in the environment in order  to try to change people's behavior to  shape up people's behavior say that it's  healthier involves making lifts less  convenient so if you slow down the speed  at which lift doors close then  eventually people get fed up and they'll  use the stairs  one of the barriers to implementing this  approach changing changing environments  in order to change behavior is that most  of our economies are built on  consumption some would say excessive  consumption say consumption of tobacco  food alcohol fossil-fueled transport say  attempting to you reduce consumption of  these products is certainly going to  improve health but it's going to  challenge economies that are  traditionally built on this focusing on  the automatic basis too much behavior is  quite a threat to how many people think  about human action most people think  that they are in control of their  behavior that what they do should not be  controlled by others by governments  regulating to change their environments  I think that much could be done by  increasing people's understanding of  psychology and neuroscience say that  people didn't see this a threat but had  just a better understanding of our  behavior say that they were able to see  that some attempts to change  environments and not trying to control  people but actually providing them with  a better environment in which to realize  what our values and goals that most of  us hold very dear ie being healthy  you
 

